# BIPOD-RKNN

Batch image processor for Rockchip RK3568 (0.8 TOPS NPU)

Runs YOLO11 and YOLOv5 on the RK3568 NPU. Requires `rknn-toolkit-lite2` 2.5.0
and `librknnrt.so` 2.5.0 on the board.

Part of [AI BIPOD](https://github.com/baboeska/bipod)

## Performance
| Platform | Model | Throughput |
|---|---|---|
| RK3568 NPU (0.8 TOPS) | YOLO11s INT8 | ~6.5 img/s |

## Honest assessment

Best as a project rather than a daily driver — the Debian 13 image ships without
GL/GLES/V4L2 acceleration, so the desktop is sluggish. If you own an RK3568,
Armbian with an open kernel is more usable overall. Run the sorter headless
over SSH.

Intended use: a second layer on MotionEye or other security camera/PVR
software, auto-sorting detections into folders.

## IMPORTANT: separate venv

`rknn-toolkit-lite2` pins numpy≤1.26.4, protobuf≤4.25.4, torch≤2.4.0.
Do NOT install it in the same venv as openvino-env or rfdetr-env — the
dependency versions are fundamentally incompatible.

## Requirements

- Python 3.12
- `rknn-toolkit-lite2` 2.5.0 (Rockchip vendor tool — not bundled, install from Rockchip)
- `librknnrt.so` 2.5.0 (runtime, must be on the board)
- opencv-python, numpy, tqdm
- setuptools<81 (newer versions break the toolkit's build)

## Setup

### 1. System packages

```bash

sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y build-essential cmake libopencv-dev
sudo usermod -aG video,render,disk $USER   # then log out and back in

2. Python environment
bash

sudo apt install -y python3.12 python3.12-venv
python3.12 -m venv ~/npu_env
source ~/npu_env/bin/activate
pip install opencv-python numpy tqdm "setuptools<81"

3. Install rknn-toolkit-lite2

The wheel is architecture-specific (aarch64) and must be installed on the board,
not copied from another machine.
bash

pip install /path/to/rknn_toolkit_lite2-2.5.0-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_17_aarch64.manylinux2014_aarch64.whl

4. Verify the runtime
bash

python3 -c "from rknnlite.api import RKNNLite; rknn = RKNNLite(); print('RKNN Lite OK')"

If this fails with a librknnrt.so error, the runtime library is missing or the
wrong version — check librknnrt.so is present and matches 2.5.0.
Model conversion (one-time, on a more powerful machine)

RKNN conversion is heavy and best done on a desktop, not the board. Two steps:
Step 1: YOLO .pt → ONNX
bash

python3 - <<'EOF'
from ultralytics import YOLO
model = YOLO("yolo11s.pt")
model.export(format="onnx", imgsz=640)
EOF

Step 2: ONNX → RKNN (INT8, with calibration)

INT8 quantization is mandatory for NPU performance. FP16 loses most of the
speed advantage.
bash

python3 - <<'EOF'
from rknn.api import RKNN
rknn = RKNN()

rknn.config(
    mean_values=[[0, 0, 0]],
    std_values=[[255, 255, 255]],
    target_platform="rk3568",
)

rknn.load_onnx(model="yolo11s.onnx")
rknn.build(do_quantization=True, dataset="calibration_dataset.txt")
rknn.export_rknn("yolo11s.rknn")
EOF

calibration_dataset.txt is a plain text file listing ~100 representative
images, one path per line.
Usage
bash

source ~/npu_env/bin/activate
python processor.py -i ~/Pictures -o ~/Pictures_Sorted

At the end of a run you get a class summary and a failures.csv log.
Known pitfalls

    INT8 only. FP16 RKNN on RK3568 loses the NPU speed advantage — quantize.

    No GL/GLES/V4L2 acceleration in the Debian 13 image. Desktop is sluggish;
    that's expected. Run headless.

    rknn-toolkit-lite2 and rknn-toolkit2 are different packages. Lite2 is
    runtime-only (for the board); toolkit2 is conversion (for the desktop).

    setuptools<81 — newer setuptools breaks the toolkit's build.

    Verify the runtime version matches. A model converted with toolkit2 2.5.0
    may not load on a board running an older librknnrt.so.

